Track 3: Biased Tape for an Unopened Will
- Story & Context: By the time maintenance crews forced open the rusted door of an abandoned postal carriage at the Doncaster Siding, the local commuter lore had already turned Mallow’s last night-train jam into a persistent urban myth. The air inside tasted bitterly of stale chicory coffee and ozone, yet the salvage team found three pocket sequencers still ticking on a solar trickle-charger, driving an automated solenoid that struck a cast-iron cowbell against the carriage frame every fourth beat. Scrawled across dozens of waterlogged, undelivered probate letters was the single chorus hook the transit workers now claim to hear through the tunnel vents on late-night routes. Sung with haughty vibrato over screeching rail-brake samples, the recording declared: "Our words keep circling on the rusted gauge."
